{{also|neuralgią}}
==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|NL.|neuralgia}}, from {{der|en|grc|νεῦρον||nerve}} + {{m|grc|ἄλγος||pain}}; synchronically, {{affix|en|neur-|-algia}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|pathology|neurology}} An acute, severe, intermittent [[pain]] that radiates along a [[nerve]].
# An acute, severe, reoccurring [[emotional]] [[distress]]. 
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|first=Philip|last=Stephens|title=Neuralgia clashes with exceptionalism in Northern Ireland|url=https://www.ft.com/content/8e158e6f-4209-424f-9381-4e3a4eec434b|accessdate=2023-02-16|newspaper=Financial Times|date=2022-12-8|text=A century later, the UK’s post-Brexit dispute with Brussels about Northern Ireland’s trading arrangements with the EU has stirred the same '''neuralgia'''.}} 
#* {{quote-web|1=en|title=Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment, December 26|date=2022-12-26|first1=Kateryna|last1=Stepanenko|first2=Karolina|last2=Hird|first3=George|last3=Barros|author4=Frederick W. Kagan|url=https://www.understandingwar.org/backgrounder/russian-offensive-campaign-assessment-december-26|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221227044500/https://www.understandingwar.org/backgrounder/russian-offensive-campaign-assessment-december-26|archivedate=2022-12-27|pu7blisher=Institute for the Study of War|work=understandingwar.org|text=Ukrainian strikes on legitimate military targets far in the Russian rear continue to be points of '''neuralgia''' for the Russian [[milblogger]] community.}}

